# Where the vendored anytls module came from.
#
# setup-anytls.sh (upstream name: install-anytls.sh), the sing-box binary in
# the repository root, and sing-box.version were copied from the Anytsl-Serve
# repository at the tag below. Three renamed constants let both projects
# run on one host without overwriting each other:
#
#   INSTALL_DIR   /etc/sing-box-anytls          -> /etc/vps-server-anytls
#   BIN_PATH      /usr/local/bin/sing-box       -> /usr/local/bin/sing-box-vps-server
#   SERVICE_NAME  sing-box-anytls.service       -> vps-server-anytls.service
#
# Other local changes:
#
#   * LOCAL_BIN, because the binary sits in the repository root while the
#     script sits in anytls/.
#   * a guard that refuses to install a second anytls inbound while
#     Anytsl-Serve's own service is running.
#   * install_singbox() tests `-f "$LOCAL_BIN"` where upstream tested `-x`.
#     `install -m 0755` sets the destination mode regardless, so requiring the
#     source to be executable buys nothing — and the executable bit does not
#     survive a CIFS/SMB working copy, which is where this repository is
#     maintained. With `-x` the script reported "binary not found" for a file
#     that was sitting right there, which is how the first anytls install
#     failed. The error message now also distinguishes absent from unusable.
#   * close_firewall() extracted from uninstall()'s inline block, plus
#     current_port() and a `reset` subcommand. Reset rotates the port and
#     password with the certificate left alone, so the SNI survives; it
#     withdraws the old port's rule before opening the new one, which upstream
#     never needed because upstream only ever opened a port or uninstalled
#     entirely. The console's reset button calls this rather than
#     reimplementing it.
#   * open_firewall() discards `iptables -C` stdout as well as stderr.
#     iptables-nft prints the matching rule when the check succeeds, so a raw
#     iptables line appeared in the operator-facing summary on every repeat
#     install. Upstream only redirected stderr, because on a first install the
#     rule never exists and -C always fails.
#   * print_result() writes the detected public address to
#     $INSTALL_DIR/public-ip.txt. The console shows the node's client
#     configuration for that address, and app.py makes no outbound request at
#     runtime — so the one moment the address can be learned is install time.
#     Removed with the config directory on uninstall.
#
# The environment variable names (ANYTLS_PORT, ANYTLS_PASSWORD, SNI, SERVER_IP)
# are deliberately NOT renamed to a VPSSRV_ prefix: renaming them would mean
# editing vendored logic, which is what this file exists to keep honest.
#
#   * uninstall() no longer unconditionally rm -f's BIN_PATH. Added 2026-09-22
#     alongside the new proxy module (proxy/setup-proxy.sh, first-party, not
#     vendored), which points its own systemd unit at the SAME vendored
#     sing-box binary rather than carrying a second copy. Uninstalling anytls
#     now checks whether /etc/vps-server-proxy/config.json still exists before
#     deleting the binary; if it does, only $INSTALL_DIR is removed. The
#     symmetric check lives in proxy/setup-proxy.sh's own uninstall().
#   * get_ip() no longer falls back to an outbound curl lookup
#     (api.ip.sb/ifconfig.me) when $SERVER_IP is unset — it returns
#     $SERVER_IP or nothing. Changed 2026-09-22: an operator testing a real
#     install found the node page showing the same IP twice (once as
#     "public", once as a LAN interface) on a VPS with no NAT, since the
#     auto-detected address there is just whatever the main interface
#     already has. print_result() now also skips any later address source
#     that repeats one an earlier source already reported, and only writes
#     (or removes) public-ip.txt based on whether $SERVER_IP was given.
#
#   * Operator-facing messages now use eight native shell catalogs under
#     lang/anytls/. The installed module requires that catalog directory,
#     including on module-only installs; protocol configuration and commands
#     remain unchanged.
#
# Refreshing this vendored tree means re-copying from a newer upstream tag,
# reapplying exactly those changes (including the proxy-awareness and
# get_ip() ones above, which upstream Anytsl-Serve has no reason to carry),
# and updating this file in the SAME commit.

Anytsl-Serve v1.2.0
